om | Operational Remedy | Failure Threshold]. Each remedy needs a `{formula}` and a failure threshold. Source material will be failure cases and warnings. A reduced count (5-8 errors) is allowed only for niche sub-domains, with documented justification.\n\nSection §7 defines ≥5 field-switching variables (Market Stage, Deal Size/Company, Product Nature, Channel, Budget). For each variable, I need to specify which rules change and which remain constant upon a variable shift.\n\n\n§8 requires 15-25 verbatim quotes, directly sourced and cited, to solidify principles. The requirement for verbatim quotes is absolute, prohibiting any modification. \"لا ينطبق\" is never permitted here. §9 demands 5-9 genuine tensions, representing polar trade-offs that resist simplistic solutions.\n\n\nSection §10 requires 4-6 ready-to-use executable templates (message texts, scripts, question matrices). Each template must be within a code block and include a \"failure threshold\" defining when it's unusable. Source materials are real-world scripts and examples. \"لا ينطبق\" is not permissible here.\n\nSection §11 demands a procedural checklist.\n\n\nAnalyzing §11 requirements, a procedural checklist must be constructed.  The checklist will outline step-by-step procedures.\n\n§12 mandates 4-5 stress test scenarios. Each scenario will contain: a situation description, conflicting options (A and B with associated costs), rejected option and rationale, chosen decision, and an impact metric. These scenarios will exceed 200 words each.\n\n§13 requires a tools list, including supporting tools and decay rates, to facilitate operations.\n\n\nSection §14 requires defining 4-6 KPIs, outlining the causal chain from lagging indicators to actionable steps, including Goodhart's Law implications.  Each KPI needs a baseline, measurement cycle, actionable trigger, and a \"decay limit\" defining when its tracking becomes counterproductive. I must locate the sector's standard success and failure metrics withi